Definition:
JDBC (Java Database Connectivity) is a standard Java API that allows Java applications to connect to relational databases, execute SQL queries, and process results.

Why JDBC?:
Database-independent API
Direct SQL execution

Foundation for:
Spring JDBC
Hibernate / JPA
MyBatis

JDBC Architecture:
Java Application
JDBC API
JDBC Driver

Database
JDBC Driver Types:
| Type   | Description                      |
| ------ | -------------------------------- |
| Type 1 | JDBC-ODBC bridge (deprecated)    |
| Type 2 | Native API (partially Java)      |
| Type 3 | Middleware                       |
| Type 4 | **Pure Java driver (most used)** |

Core JDBC Components:
1. DriverManager:
Manages database drivers and connections.

2. Connection:
Represents a connection to the database.
Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, pass);

3. Statement:
Used to execute SQL queries.

Types:
Statement
PreparedStatement
CallableStatement

4. ResultSet:
Holds data returned from SELECT query.

JDBC Workflow:
Load driver
Create connection
Create statement
Execute query
Process result
Close resources

JDBC Example (Basic)
Table:
CREATE TABLE employee (
  id INT,
  name VARCHAR(50)
);

Java Code:
import java.sql.*;

public class JdbcDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/test";
        String user = "root";
        String pass = "root";

        Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, pass);
        Statement stmt = con.createStatement();

        ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery("SELECT * FROM employee");

        while (rs.next()) {
            System.out.println(rs.getInt("id") + " " + rs.getString("name"));
        }

        con.close();
    }
}

Output:
1 Sai
2 Ram

Statement vs PreparedStatement (VERY IMPORTANT)
Statement:
Statement stmt = con.createStatement();
stmt.executeQuery("SELECT * FROM employee WHERE id=1");


❌ SQL Injection risk
❌ Slow (query compiled every time)

PreparedStatement:
PreparedStatement ps =
    con.prepareStatement("SELECT * FROM employee WHERE id=?");
ps.setInt(1, 1);


✔ Prevents SQL Injection
✔ Faster (pre-compiled)
✔ Recommended

PreparedStatement Example:
PreparedStatement ps =
    con.prepareStatement("INSERT INTO employee VALUES (?, ?)");

ps.setInt(1, 3);
ps.setString(2, "John");
ps.executeUpdate();

Output:
1 row inserted

CallableStatement (Stored Procedure)
Stored Procedure:
CREATE PROCEDURE getEmployee()
BEGIN
  SELECT * FROM employee;
END;

Java Code:
CallableStatement cs = con.prepareCall("{call getEmployee()}");
ResultSet rs = cs.executeQuery();

JDBC Transactions
Default Behavior:

Auto-commit = true

Manual Transaction:
con.setAutoCommit(false);

try {
    stmt.executeUpdate("UPDATE account SET bal=bal-100 WHERE id=1");
    stmt.executeUpdate("UPDATE account SET bal=bal+100 WHERE id=2");
    con.commit();
} catch (Exception e) {
    con.rollback();
}

Batch Processing (Performance)
PreparedStatement ps =
    con.prepareStatement("INSERT INTO employee VALUES (?, ?)");

ps.setInt(1, 4);
ps.setString(2, "A");
ps.addBatch();

ps.setInt(1, 5);
ps.setString(2, "B");
ps.addBatch();

ps.executeBatch();

Output:
Batch executed successfully

